1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,107
A file that a user uploads is untrusted input.

2
00:00:04,107 --> 00:00:08,647
This video shows how Orchard Core checks it before storing it, how the

3
00:00:08,647 --> 00:00:13,367
ClamAV Antivirus Scanner rejects a test file, and how your own module can

4
00:00:13,367 --> 00:00:14,920
use the same checks.

5
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:24,118
Orchard Core runs a pipeline on an upload before the file is stored.

6
00:00:24,118 --> 00:00:30,206
FileCreationService passes the stream to each IFileEventHandler in turn, and any handler can

7
00:00:30,206 --> 00:00:32,940
reject the file or replace the stream.

8
00:00:33,690 --> 00:00:39,346
A handler returns a successful result to continue, or a failed result to stop the upload.

9
00:00:39,346 --> 00:00:43,872
Only a file that passes is written to the store, and then CreatedAsync

10
00:00:43,872 --> 00:00:46,670
tells every handler that the file exists.

11
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:52,217
The Media upload API behind the media library, the Media recipe step, Package

12
00:00:52,217 --> 00:00:55,222
Import and remote instance import all call it.

13
00:00:55,222 --> 00:01:00,020
Resumable uploads from the Media TUS Uploads feature, and direct calls to the

14
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:01,580
media store, skip it.

15
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:09,756
The ClamAV Antivirus Scanner feature adds one handler.

16
00:01:09,756 --> 00:01:13,400
It streams each file to a clamd service over TCP.

17
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:18,303
A detection rejects the upload, and so does any failure to reach the

18
00:01:18,303 --> 00:01:20,490
scanner or read its answer.

19
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:28,301
Configure the connection under OrchardCore, Antivirus, ClamAV, or with environment variables.

20
00:01:28,301 --> 00:01:32,865
Host has no default, so until you set it every scan fails.

21
00:01:32,865 --> 00:01:39,070
Port defaults to 3310, and the connect and transfer timeouts to 5 and 30 seconds.

22
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:43,226
The handler rewinds the stream before and after the scan.

23
00:01:43,226 --> 00:01:47,119
A stream that cannot seek is first copied to a temporary file from

24
00:01:47,119 --> 00:01:51,120
ITempDirectoryProvider, which is deleted when that stream is closed.

25
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:58,803
The scanner is configured, so let's enable it.

26
00:01:58,803 --> 00:02:04,657
In Tools, Features, the ClamAV Antivirus Scanner is listed in the Security category.

27
00:02:04,657 --> 00:02:07,780
Let's search for ClamAV and select Enable.

28
00:02:08,315 --> 00:02:12,873
The site restarts its features, and the ClamAV Antivirus Scanner row now shows

29
00:02:12,873 --> 00:02:15,314
the Enabled badge and a Disable button.

30
00:02:15,314 --> 00:02:20,795
There is nothing else to set in the admin, because the connection comes from the configuration.

31
00:02:22,495 --> 00:02:28,131
In Media, Library, Upload files sends the files you choose to the Media upload API.

32
00:02:28,131 --> 00:02:31,805
Let's upload a small spreadsheet of office supplies.

33
00:02:32,325 --> 00:02:34,763
The file appears in the library.

34
00:02:34,763 --> 00:02:39,575
ClamAV answered OK for it, so the upload continued to the file store.

35
00:02:41,275 --> 00:02:44,418
Let's upload a standard antivirus test file next.

36
00:02:44,418 --> 00:02:48,475
It is harmless, and antivirus scanners are designed to detect it.

37
00:02:48,926 --> 00:02:50,723
The upload is rejected.

38
00:02:50,723 --> 00:02:55,434
The Uploads panel counts one error, and its message names the file and

39
00:02:55,434 --> 00:02:57,976
the signature that ClamAV detected.

40
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:02,259
The test file was never stored, so it is not in the library.

41
00:03:02,259 --> 00:03:04,390
Clear Errors empties the panel.

42
00:03:06,090 --> 00:03:10,664
The scanner checks each file that arrives at the Media upload API.

43
00:03:10,664 --> 00:03:15,620
With the clamd service stopped, let's upload a second clean spreadsheet.

44
00:03:16,098 --> 00:03:22,125
The upload is rejected again, and the message names the ClamAV antivirus scanner and the file.

45
00:03:22,125 --> 00:03:27,428
A scanner that cannot answer stops the upload instead of letting the file through.

46
00:03:31,628 --> 00:03:37,059
Code that stores an uploaded file itself calls FileCreationService before the write.

47
00:03:37,059 --> 00:03:42,610
CreateAsync takes a context and the stream, and returns a FileCreatingResult that owns

48
00:03:42,610 --> 00:03:45,928
the final stream, so declare it with await using.

49
00:03:46,678 --> 00:03:49,099
If the result did not succeed, stop.

50
00:03:49,099 --> 00:03:52,961
Otherwise store the processed stream, then call CreatedAsync.

51
00:03:52,961 --> 00:03:55,151
Scan before the permanent write:

52
00:03:55,151 --> 00:03:59,588
a file that is stored first and scanned afterward is already persisted.

53
00:04:00,338 --> 00:04:06,913
FileCreationService is registered by the Media, Deployment and Remote Deployment modules with TryAddTransient,

54
00:04:06,913 --> 00:04:10,314
so a module that depends on none of them registers it.

55
00:04:10,314 --> 00:04:15,585
In the media store, call the CreateFileFromStreamAsync overload that takes the service.

56
00:04:15,585 --> 00:04:17,568
The plain call does not scan.

57
00:04:18,318 --> 00:04:21,574
A handler of your own implements IFileEventHandler.

58
00:04:21,574 --> 00:04:26,429
Return Failed from CreatingAsync, with an error message, to stop the upload, or

59
00:04:26,429 --> 00:04:28,828
Success with the stream to continue.

60
00:04:29,578 --> 00:04:35,730
Register the handler as a singleton with TryAddEnumerable, as the ClamAV feature does,

61
00:04:35,730 --> 00:04:39,006
and every FileCreationService call runs it.

62
00:04:39,006 --> 00:04:44,288
A handler can also throw, as ClamAV does when the scanner is unreachable.

63
00:04:48,488 --> 00:04:53,625
A chunked upload in progress, a resumable upload on the default disk store,

64
00:04:53,625 --> 00:04:58,634
an extracted deployment archive and a recipe sent to the tenant setup API

65
00:04:58,634 --> 00:05:04,468
each ask ITempDirectoryProvider for a path, not the operating system temporary folder.

66
00:05:05,218 --> 00:05:10,373
By default, files go to OrchardCore, Temp, in the user's local application data

67
00:05:10,373 --> 00:05:12,981
folder, in one sub-folder per tenant.

68
00:05:12,981 --> 00:05:18,438
Set Path in the OrchardCore, TempDirectory section to use a larger or shared volume.

69
00:05:19,188 --> 00:05:25,484
The path must be a real file system, because consumers seek inside the files.

70
00:05:25,484 --> 00:05:30,338
A mounted file share such as Azure Files or AWS EFS works.

71
00:05:30,338 --> 00:05:34,358
Azure Blob and Amazon S3 object storage do not.

72
00:05:35,108 --> 00:05:38,303
In your own code, inject ITempDirectoryProvider.

73
00:05:38,303 --> 00:05:42,621
Its members return the tenant root folder, a unique file path, or a

74
00:05:42,621 --> 00:05:45,756
sub-folder that they create with a unique name.

75
00:05:45,756 --> 00:05:51,138
You create, write and delete the files yourself, and the provider does not scan them.

76
00:05:53,138 --> 00:05:58,694
Uploads that call FileCreationService are checked before storage, the ClamAV scanner is one

77
00:05:58,694 --> 00:06:05,279
handler in that pipeline, and ITempDirectoryProvider hands out temporary paths in one tenant-scoped location.

78
00:06:05,279 --> 00:06:06,768
Thanks for watching.

